From 1d1f34692313057a089ff4a37270b9822786820f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lucascouts <lucascs@protonmail.com>
Date: Sun, 27 Sep 2026 00:52:14 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] sidebar: show a thread whose turn ended with background work
 still running

The sidebar's thread icon only knew two live states: a turn in flight (the
grey spinner) and everything else. Claude Code runs work that outlives the
turn that launched it -- a `run_in_background` shell, an async subagent, a
monitor -- and once the prompt returns ACP has nothing to say about it:
AcpThread::status() is `running_turn.is_some()`, so the row went back to the
agent's plain icon while the work carried on, and only a later follow-up turn
brought the spinner back.

The adapter already tracked this. claude-agent-acp-plus consumes the SDK's
`background_tasks_changed` level to decide when a turn may settle; it now
forwards the same level, filtered of the tasks the SDK marks `ambient` ("not
activity"), as `session_info_update._meta["_claude/backgroundTasks"]` =
`{ count, tasks: [{ id, type, description }] }`, and only when the set
changes. Measured over ACP stdio against the real CLI on 2026-09-27: the
prompt answered at 4.9 s, `count: 1` had arrived at 3.9 s, and `count: 0`
arrived at 23.9 s -- outside any turn, which is the case this patch exists for.

What this adds:

- AcpThread reads the count off `session_info_update` and emits
  `BackgroundTasksUpdated` when it changes. An update without the key leaves
  the count alone: the adapter's title-only updates ride the same
  notification, and reading their silence as "none left" would clear it.
- `AgentThreadStatus::Background`, chosen when the turn is idle and the count
  is non-zero. It ranks below waiting-for-confirmation and error, exactly as
  Running does, so an alert is never hidden behind it.
- The icon is the same LoadCircle as a running turn, tinted Accent and turning
  at half the speed, so it still reads as "working" but the two stay apart.
  A tooltip names it: "Running in the Background".
- The "finished while you were away" dot now also fires on
  Background -> Completed, so the end of the last background task is news the
  way the end of a turn is.

What it deliberately does not do:

- It routes nothing through ThreadMetadataStore. The first draft marked the
  event as `affects_thread_metadata`, on the theory that the store was the only
  path to the sidebar; a mutation test showed the sidebar refreshed without
  it. Every thread event ends in `cx.notify()` on the ConversationView, and the
  agent panel observes every conversation view -- active or not -- and asks
  the sidebar to rebuild. The metadata route would have rewritten `updated_at`
  in the database twice per background task for nothing.
- Stop treats Background like Completed. "Stop Generation" cancels a turn,
  and there is no turn to cancel; offering it would promise to kill work it
  cannot reach.
- It does not let a Background thread be archived. Since 0.6.1_p20261021 it
  follows upstream, which refuses to archive a running or Unconfirmed
  thread: ArchiveSelectedThread returns on Background too, and the row shows
  no archive button, because archiving would hide a thread whose work still
  runs. The row's context menu still offers Archive Thread, as upstream's
  does for a running thread.
- The collapsed project header's spinner is unchanged: it still means a turn
  is running.

No other agent sends the key, so for them nothing changes. Rides the
claude-agent-acp-plus flag BY DEPENDENCY, like 0011 and 0013.

Test added (0.2.3_p20261009): a malformed count (-1, "2", 1.5, null) leaves the last one.
Tests added 2026-10-10 (story 032 v4): Background -> Completed marks a pinned row and header.
Test added (0.6.1_p20261021): archiving a Background thread is refused.

a4b39c38 review: (2026-11-01, story 072) Upstream #66255 now reports native
subagent state the way it reports ACP subagents, through update_session_state,
restore_subagent_outcomes and the linked session messages of #66101/#66102. This
patch reads its _meta in the SessionInfoUpdate arm of
AcpThread::handle_session_update, an arm upstream did not touch. Upstream
changed only the SessionMessage arm there. No new dispatch path bypasses
handle_session_update.
---

